ion": "IL-6", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9764993786811829, "span": {"begin": 688, "end": 692}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "acute phase response genes", "obj": "DNA", "prob": 0.9057777523994446, "span": {"begin": 812, "end": 838}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:7124"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "TNF", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9940301179885864, "span": {"begin": 890, "end": 893}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3569"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "IL-6", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.980927586555481, "span": {"begin": 895, "end": 899}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3553"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "IL-1B", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9960120916366577, "span": {"begin": 904, "end": 909}}], "text": "The biological plausibility is high. After cells sense pathogens, tissue damage or dysmetabolism, production of acute phase proteins (KE 1438) is triggered by cellular pattern-recognition molecules, through a cytokine cascade (Mantovani &amp; Garlanda, 2023). Specifically, IL-6, TNF and IL-1b are known transcriptional inducers of acute phase response gene transcription (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). &nbsp;In the lungs, the cytokine cascade is produced by epithelial cells and resident macrophages (KE1495) (Moldoveanu et al., 2009).   &nbsp;  Thus, pulmonary exposure to diesel engine exhaust in female mice led to increased levels of several cytokines including CCL2, CXCL1, IL-1B and IL-6 24 h post-exposure (Bendtsen et al., 2020; McCarrick et al., 2025). This was accompanied by increased transcription of acute phase response genes in lung tissue (Gliga et al., 2026). Among others, TNF, IL-6 and IL-1B were identified as upstream regulators of pulmonary transcription (Gliga et al., 2026).", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:48:37 +0000 2026"}